{"name":"Entrapment","hex":"#005961","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"entrapment","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The state of being entrapped.","Action by law enforcement personnel to lead an otherwise innocent person to commit a crime, in order to arrest and prosecute that person for the crime."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/entrapment"}],"entries":[{"title":"Entrapment","blocks":[{"text":"Entrapment is a practice in which a law enforcement agent or an agent of the state induces a person to commit a crime that the person would have otherwise been unlikely or unwilling to commit. In US law, it is defined as \"the conception and planning of an offense by an officer or agent, and the procurement of its commission by one who would not have perpetrated it except for the trickery, persuasion or fraud of the officer or state agent\"."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Entrapment"},{"title":"Entrapment (film)","blocks":[{"text":"Entrapment is a 1999 heist film directed by Jon Amiel and written by Ronald Bass. It stars Sean Connery and Catherine Zeta-Jones and includes Will Patton, Ving Rhames and Maury Chaykin. The film focuses on the relationship between an investigator and a professional thief as they attempt a heist at the turn of the millennium."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/8/89/Entrapment_film.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Entrapment_(film)"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b1/Malaysia_KL_1.JPG/500px-Malaysia_KL_1.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b1/Malaysia_KL_1.JPG/500px-Malaysia_KL_1.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The Petronas Twin Towers, where the final heist scene takes place.","link":"http